{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789817611,"nonce":"qnf1sQUjLX7sIxU6q3ACi4QAEy0=","title":"a local named like an abstract's static is redirected to the static","message":"Inside `haxe.Int64Helper.fromFloat(f:Float):Int64`, `var neg = noFractions \u003c 0; var rest = neg ? -noFractions : noFractions;` reads `neg` as `haxe.Int64.neg` (the `@:op(-A)` static), so the ternary lowers to nothing, `rest` is never bound, and the function body after it is `unreachable`. LLVM then folds the tail into the guard's throw: `Int64.fromFloat(3.0)` raises \"Number is NaN or Infinite\". `Int64.parseString` is the same shape.\n\nThe identifier resolution in `ast_lowering/expr/mod.rs` redirects a bare name to a static of the EXPECTED abstract type (enum-abstract constants, `unit.Bar` vs `Foo.Bar`). The expected type falls back to the enclosing function's declared return type, so in any function returning `Int64` every bare name matching an Int64 static -- `neg`, `add`, `compare`, `make`... -- is rewritten, locals included.\n\nFix: a local or parameter shadows the redirect, and only a non-function static qualifies (an abstract's static methods are never bare names in Haxe).\n\n`RAYZOR_LOWER_TRACE=1` prints the `[lower-none] variable neg(Function, haxe.Int64.neg)` line that located this.","files":null}]}